---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title="What's the failure mode of this cache.fetch under high traffic?" description="Practice card. Rails.cache.fetch looks fine in dev. In production it breaks in a specific shape. Drills cache stampede.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Scaling · Card 3</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">What's the failure mode of this cache under high traffic?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">The code is straight from the Rails Guides. It works perfectly in development. In production, under load, it has a specific failure mode.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">A homepage controller, getting 5,000 requests per minute.</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`def index
  @posts = Rails.cache.fetch("homepage_feed", expires_in: 1.minute) do
    Post.includes(:user, :tags)
        .order(score: :desc)
        .limit(100)
        .to_a
  end
end

# In a hot loop: 5,000 req/min, 60-second TTL.`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Under sustained traffic, this code starts behaving badly every minute. Which option names the failure mode?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'Cache key collision — another part of the app is using the same key.',
              explanation: 'Possible in theory but it would show up as wrong data, not a periodic slowdown. Use a unique key prefix per cache type and you\'re fine.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: 'Cache stampede — when the cache expires, hundreds of in-flight requests all miss at once and rebuild simultaneously.',
              explanation: 'Every 60 seconds, the entry expires. The next batch of concurrent requests all see "cache miss" before any of them finishes rebuilding. They all run the expensive query against the database at the same time, multiplying the cost by however many concurrent requests landed in that window. The fix: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">race_condition_ttl</code>, jittered TTLs, or a separate warm-keeping job.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'The TTL is too short for Rails.cache to be effective.',
              explanation: 'TTL choice is a tradeoff (freshness vs hit rate), not a failure mode. A 1-minute TTL is reasonable for a feed that needs to feel live.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'Rails.cache.fetch doesn\'t support blocks; it should use <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">read</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">write</code> separately.',
              explanation: 'It does support blocks; that\'s the whole point of the <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">fetch</code> API. The block is the "what to do on miss."',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>Cache stampede (sometimes called "thundering herd") happens when many requests miss the cache simultaneously and all decide to rebuild. The cache exists to spare the database; the stampede makes the database take the hit anyway, but in an even worse pattern: synchronized concurrent expensive queries.</p><p>Three real fixes:</p><ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4 space-y-1"><li><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">race_condition_ttl:</code> tells Rails to extend an expiring entry briefly so only one request rebuilds while the others keep serving the slightly-stale value.</li><li>Jitter the TTL: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">expires_in: 1.minute + rand(10).seconds</code>. Spreads expirations across requests.</li><li>Warm the cache from a background job. The web request never sees a miss.</li></ul>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/senior-scaling-caching"
          theoryLabel="Scaling · Caching, Properly"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-scaling-n-plus-one-count" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 2</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-scaling-offset-pagination" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 4 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
